WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.531
There is no single best local language model for every user.

00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:16.371
A useful choice depends on the task, hardware, memory, speed, context, output quality, concurrency, privacy, and operational tolerance for slow or unstable behavior.

00:00:16.371 --> 00:00:20.132
Local inference is a hardware-constrained engineering decision.

00:00:20.132 --> 00:00:30.087
The complete configuration includes model architecture, parameter count, quantization, context size, runtime, acceleration backend, offload, prompt template, generation settings, and cooling.

00:00:30.087 --> 00:00:37.830
A model that leads one public benchmark may perform poorly on a specific machine or task.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:42.918
Benchmarks use particular prompts, scoring methods, hardware, software, and settings.

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:48.891
They are useful for forming a shortlist, not declaring a permanent winner.

00:00:48.891 --> 00:00:57.961
Quality has several dimensions: factuality, instruction following, coding ability, writing style, domain performance, refusal behavior, hallucination risk, and consistency.

00:00:57.961 --> 00:01:04.819
A larger score or parameter count does not guarantee the behavior an application needs.

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:07.695
Performance also has multiple dimensions.

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:11.899
Time to first token matters in interactive use.

00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:14.775
Generation speed affects reading pace.

00:01:14.775 --> 00:01:18.093
Prompt processing matters for long documents.

00:01:18.093 --> 00:01:23.181
Throughput and stability matter for batch jobs and multiple users.

00:01:23.181 --> 00:01:32.252
The best choice is the smallest or simplest configuration that meets the real requirement with acceptable quality and margin.

00:01:32.252 --> 00:01:40.437
Start with the task and machine, test representative work, and scale only when measured shortcomings justify it.

00:01:40.437 --> 00:01:49.507
Include total operating cost in the decision: hardware purchase, electricity, cooling, storage, engineering time, maintenance, downtime, and review effort.

00:01:49.507 --> 00:01:54.596
A free model can still be expensive to operate badly.
